  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1. A cold airmass continues this weekend. Another round of \nfrost is possible tonight into Monday morning.\n\nThe Freeze Warning expired at 9 AM. Temperatures early this morning\ndropped to or below freezing across portions of eastern Pennsylvania\nand northern New Jersey. Given the widespread freezing temperatures\nobserved, the 2026 growing season has ended for the following counties...\n\nIn Pennsylvania...Carbon, Monroe and Northampton counties.\n\nIn New Jersey...Sussex and Warren counties.\n\nNo additional frost/freeze headlines will be issued for these counties\nuntil Spring 2027.\n\nSunny and chilly today with highs about 10 degrees below average,\ntopping off mostly in the upper 50s. \n\nAnother cold night on tap for tonight, and additional frost and/or\nfreeze headlines may be needed for areas where the growing season\ncontinues.\n\nTemperatures will then begin to slowly moderate into early this week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Uncertainty persists regarding a Nor'easter \ndeveloping off the coast of the southern Mid-Atlantic that may \ncause unsettled conditions and hazardous marine impacts early \nnext week.\n\nThe base of a deep trough axis will pass through the region late\ntonight into Sunday. As it does so, the trough axis will then \ncutoff into an upper level low which will meander over the \nsouthern Mid-Atlantic coast into early next week. At the same \ntime, strong and expansive high pressure will be building over \nthe Ohio Valley. While confidence is increasing regarding a \nnor'easter developing off the North Carolina coast by Sunday \ninto Sunday night, it must be said there remains a vast amount \nof uncertainty with regards to potential impacts to our region \ninto next week. \n\nConsiderable differences amongst global/ensemble guidance \nexists with regards to just how close/long this system will \nget/last in our region. Relative to the previous forecast \ndiscussion, there has not been much change. The EC ensemble \nmember spread is fairly tightly clustered through the early part\nof next week, and then begins to diverge, but generally has the\nlow tracking northward and then meandering off the Mid-Atlantic\ncoast into mid or even late week. The GEFS members are similar \nuntil about mid-week when they begin to diverge more \nsubstantially. All of this being said, it doesn't appear likely \nthat there will be significant impacts inland from this system, \neven though they can't be entirely ruled out at this time. What \nis increasingly likely though is at least some impacts to \ncoastal communities with periods of gusty northeast winds, some \nshowers, and potentially some coastal flooding concerns, \npotentially for multiple days next week. We will continue to \nmonitor the progress of this developing system.",
